STYLE: A grand hand-made toy theatre production built from printed card. Every piece is a flat cutout of thick card about 4 mm thick, cut by hand with a craft knife, with raw brown card at the edges. The face is painted in soft charcoal line and grey ink wash with thin watercolour on warm Korean hanji mulberry paper, its long fibres visible. Loose expressive charcoal strokes and quiet, tender faces. Broad, calm shapes readable from the back row. Puppet joints are round brass split pins. The present day has the last amber and persimmon light of dusk over indigo-grey water; the memories are cool and rain-washed. Palette: hanji cream #EDE6D6, charcoal #2B2A28, slate #5E6670, mist #C9CDD0, earth #6E5A44, indigo #3B4A66, pine #4E5E48, persimmon #B4553A, dusk amber #D6A365, raw card #A57E52. The mood is family love, regret and unanswered longing in Korea, many years after a separation in June 1950.
CONSTRAINTS: No text, writing, lettering, numbers, logos, signature or watermark. No national flags, insignia, emblems, armbands or badges. No violence or weapons in use. No real persons. No photographic people, plastic, glossy finish, anime style or sculpted 3D characters. The illustration must remain a painted, flat card cutout. The rods, strings, light and sky will be added by the theatre.
Use case: illustration-story.

SUBJECT: Use the attached Hyeong picture only as a reference for the charcoal-and-hanji toy-theatre style and the two brothers' faces. A new wide key-art illustration of the whole stage inside a simple dark proscenium at dusk. Near the front left stands Cheol, now an elderly Korean man with short white hair, a lined strong kind face, worn grey-brown coat, cream shirt, faded indigo scarf, dark trousers and shoes. He is a flat jointed card marionette, slightly stooped over a wooden cane, his free hand reaching towards the far bank. The river is made from two low rocking rows of cut card. A broken wooden bridgehead stands empty. At the back is the small Korean village with tiled roofs, a chimney and just a few amber windows beneath misty charcoal hills. The smooth cyclorama glows from deep blue above to muted amber at the horizon, with NO painted sky detail. Reeds frame the near bank. A small, cool, rain-washed memory of Min at nineteen, in off-white work clothes, is a separate faint card at the far left, clearly part of an earlier time; he does not answer or approach the older man. The feeling is the beauty of home remembered and the ache of an unanswered river. Strong, calm layered composition; rich amber light against indigo water. No generated text. Landscape, 1536 by 1024 or larger.
